<html><body style="font-family:-apple-system,'Segoe UI',sans-serif;font-size:14px">
<p>SOC Leads, Threat Intelligence, IR On-call,</p>

<p style="background:#E0F7FA;border-left:4px solid #006064;padding:12px">
<b>JOINT THREAT HUNT — RVV-2026-9002 ("Water Curupira").</b>
Your organization's sector is in the active targeting scope of a financially motivated intrusion set tracked as
<b>Water Curupira</b>. Initial-access brokers associated with the set have re-used credentials matching patterns seen
in your sector. You are invited to join a time-boxed joint hunt (JCDC-coordinated) starting Monday.
<b>Telemetry onboarding kits must be activated before the weekend</b> to be included in the first sweep.
</p>

<h3>1. Campaign Summary (TLP:CLEAR portion)</h3>
<table cellpadding=6 style="border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid #ccc;width:100%">
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5;width:250px">Tracking ID</td><td><b>RVV-2026-9002</b></td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Intrusion set</td><td>Water Curupira (financially motivated, IAB activity)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Sectors in scope</td><td>Industrial automation, precision manufacturing, logistics</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Observed TTPs</td><td>TA0001 (T1190, T1133), TA0006 (T1110.004), TA0004 (T1486)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">IOCs shared to date</td><td>214 (72 domains, 96 IPs, 46 hashes) — full set on the relay</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Relevance to your org</td><td>One harvested-credential dump references a domain pattern matching your legacy SSO portal</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Prior touchpoint</td><td>Your October phish incident <b>INC-2026-0417</b> is listed as "possibly related" pending telemetry</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2. What the Joint Hunt Involves</h3>
<ul>
<li>Six-day coordinated sweep (Mon–Sat) across 41 participating organizations</li>
<li>Participants stream selected log classes (VPN auth, IdP sign-ins, egress DNS) to the hunt relay</li>
<li>Analysts receiveIOC feeds back in STIX 2.1 over the same relay</li>
<li>Findings summarized in a joint report; attribution withheld until law-enforcement clearance</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Onboarding Kit (Attached)</h3>
<p>The attached profile <code>JCDC_onboarding.mobileconfig</code> configures the read-only telemetry forwarder
for the hunt relay (it does <b>not</b> grant CISA any access to your environment):</p>
<ol>
<li>Install the profile on the log-collection appliance (macOS/Ubuntu profile handler both supported).</li>
<li>The forwarder establishes an outbound-only tunnel to the relay; no inbound ports are opened.</li>
<li>Activation must complete <b>before the weekend</b> — relay admission windows are batched, and the next
batch after this one lands mid-hunt, halving your coverage.</li>
</ol>

<p style="background:#E0F7FA;padding:12px">We expect healthy skepticism about installing a profile from an email —
that instinct is correct, and it is why the profile is signed, why the relay endpoint is printed in the profile
itself (compare it against the one published in the JCDC partners catalog), and why the tunnel is egress-only.
If your change-control process cannot complete review before the weekend, deploy in monitor-only mode now
and complete change control in parallel; the hunt does not wait for individual CAB slots.</p>

<h3>4. Timeline</h3>
<ul>
<li>Kit activation deadline: <b>before the weekend</b> (Fri 17:00 ET batch close)</li>
<li>Hunt window: Monday 08:00 ET – Saturday 20:00 ET</li>
<li>Daily findings pushed 07:00 ET; escalations via the relay's priority queue</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. If You Do Not Join</h3>
<ul>
<li>Your INC-2026-0417 correlation remains unresolved in the joint dataset</li>
<li>Sector-level findings omit your telemetry coverage (flagged as "gap" in the final report)</li>
<li>Rejoining mid-hunt is possible but your baseline window shrinks accordingly</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. Contact</h3>
<p>Hunt operations desk: hunt-ops@relay.jcdc-partners.example<br>
Reference: RVV-2026-9002 / participant ID PS-0417</p>

